From: =?UTF-8?q?Filip=20Lewi=C5=84ski?= <filip.lewinski@3mdeb.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 15:23:17 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 5/9] soc/intel/common/block/gpio: add option to lock the
 PLTRST# pad
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Origin: Dasharo/coreboot `dasharo` branch commit a6df47479d3d3a5ac30d0b9d23383cd5a148460a (2026-09-08, direct push)
Upstream-Status: Pending — Gerrit mirror is review.coreboot.org topic tpm_gpio_pltrst_lock (CB:95509-95516; of which 95509/95510/95511 merged to coreboot main as of 2026-10-10); supersedes Dasharo/coreboot PR #959 (closed 2026-09-15, not merged)
Refs: "TPM GPIO fail" https://mkukri.xyz/2024-06-01/tpm-gpio-fail.html ; coreboot ticket #576 ; https://github.com/tlaurion/tpm-gpio-fail

PLTRST# is the native function of a GPIO pad on Intel PCHs. Software
running after the firmware can switch that pad to GPIO mode and drive
it, which resets a discrete TPM while the host keeps running: the PCRs
go back to their reset values and can be extended with arbitrary
measurements, so sealed secrets can be unsealed on a machine that never
went through a measured boot. See
https://mkukri.xyz/2024/06/01/tpm-gpio-fail.html

Add an option for SoCs that provide the pad in soc_gpio_lock_config(),
locking it from the SMM finalize handler, which is the only place where
the write is honored and where FSP can no longer undo it.

The option therefore depends on something issuing APM_CNT_FINALIZE.
Either trigger will do: with INTEL_CHIPSET_LOCKDOWN coreboot issues it
itself, and with SOC_INTEL_COMMON_SPI_LOCKDOWN_SMM the payload issues it
after it has set up flash protection. The two are mutually exclusive, as
SOC_INTEL_COMMON_SPI_LOCKDOWN_SMM depends on !INTEL_CHIPSET_LOCKDOWN.
Depending on INTEL_CHIPSET_LOCKDOWN alone would hide the option on
exactly the boards that need it, since a payload such as heads takes
over the lockdown and clears INTEL_CHIPSET_LOCKDOWN.

SoCs opt in by selecting SOC_INTEL_COMMON_BLOCK_GPIO_HAS_PLTRST_PAD, so
that the option stays hidden where no pad list is provided and the
lock would silently do nothing.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Filip Lewiński <filip.lewinski@3mdeb.com>
---
 src/soc/intel/common/block/gpio/Kconfig | 38 +++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 38 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/soc/intel/common/block/gpio/Kconfig b/src/soc/intel/common/block/gpio/Kconfig
index 60a5967bde..7f4c2edc00 100644
--- a/src/soc/intel/common/block/gpio/Kconfig
+++ b/src/soc/intel/common/block/gpio/Kconfig
@@ -55,6 +55,44 @@ config SOC_INTEL_COMMON_BLOCK_GPIO_LOCK_USING_PCR
 	  Starting with MTL SoC, the recommendation is to use PCR for locking down the
 	  GPIO configuration.
 
+# Selected by SoCs that return the pad driving PLTRST# from
+# soc_gpio_lock_config().
+config SOC_INTEL_COMMON_BLOCK_GPIO_HAS_PLTRST_PAD
+	bool
+
+config SOC_INTEL_COMMON_BLOCK_GPIO_LOCK_PLTRST_PAD
+	bool "Lock the PLTRST# pad configuration"
+	default y
+	depends on SOC_INTEL_COMMON_BLOCK_GPIO_HAS_PLTRST_PAD
+	depends on HAVE_SMI_HANDLER
+	depends on INTEL_CHIPSET_LOCKDOWN || SOC_INTEL_COMMON_SPI_LOCKDOWN_SMM
+	depends on SOC_INTEL_COMMON_BLOCK_GPIO_LOCK_USING_SBI || \
+		   SOC_INTEL_COMMON_BLOCK_GPIO_LOCK_USING_PCR
+	select SOC_INTEL_COMMON_BLOCK_SMM_LOCK_GPIO_PADS
+	help
+	  PLTRST# is the native function of a GPIO pad. As long as that pad
+	  configuration stays writable, system software can switch the pad to
+	  GPIO mode and drive a platform reset edge. A discrete TPM is reset by
+	  that edge while the host keeps running, so its PCRs are cleared
+	  without restarting the measured boot chain and can be replayed with
+	  arbitrary values.
+
+	  Lock the pad configuration from the SMM finalize handler, i.e. after
+	  FSP has run its notify phases, so the pad mode can no longer be
+	  changed. Only the configuration is locked. The Tx state is left
+	  writable, as it has no effect while the pad drives PLTRST#.
+
+	  The finalize handler runs when APM_CNT_FINALIZE is issued. coreboot
+	  issues it on the normal boot path when INTEL_CHIPSET_LOCKDOWN is set,
+	  and always on S3 resume, where the payload does not run. When
+	  SOC_INTEL_COMMON_SPI_LOCKDOWN_SMM defers the SPI and LPC lockdown to
+	  the payload, the payload issues it instead, so the pad configuration
+	  stays writable for as long as the payload runs.
+
+	  Say N on a board that does not use this pad for PLTRST#, or that uses
+	  it as a GPIO: locking the configuration of a pad that already is in
+	  GPIO mode does not keep software from driving it.
+
 # Indicate if SoC supports 4 bits Pad Mode with PAD_CFG_DW0 registers
 config SOC_INTEL_COMMON_BLOCK_GPIO_PMODE_4BITS
 	bool
-- 
